risks:
Metadata the design cannot hide:
- the pharmacist sees the picker; an older adult with a child's prescription suggests a grandparent;
- guardian-edge issuance coincides with a dependent's enrollment, which epoch batching only blurs;
- the sequencer sees the network origin of submissions;
- a card's key hash is a stable pseudonym across pickups;
- small networks give small anonymity sets;
- a patient with exactly one delegate gains nothing from hiding the hop count.

findings.attack:
Settlement depends on the patient wallet's preimage holder. A delegated pickup finalizes as a PendingWalletUpdate that only W_c's preimage holder can prove, and the claim leaf rides on that proof. Cases where nobody proves:
- an incapacitated adult with an AUTH_DOC representative, whose own phone sits in a drawer;
- a child whose custodial phone is lost;
- a parent in hospital, which is the very situation delegation exists for.
Every delegated fill also makes the custodial phone take the D7 proving lease, which D8 has not yet hardened against hogging. A parent with three wards holds it three times.

relationship_leaks_to:
Custodial parent, about the minor's confidential care:
- reads claims as 'self' with W_c's key, or through a W_c attestation it can mint at will (l2-rpc-server.ts:2906-2926);
- receives the 'fill' wallet update it must prove before the claim lands (l2-sequencer-service.ts:1430-1449);
- sees the planned '1 confidential item' line.

Pharmacy:
- presenterMode separates the patient (0) from a holder (1); card and code modes mark a delegate without a phone;
- the pseudonym comes only from the holder secret, so one parent shows the same pseudonym on every sibling's prescription, which reveals the household;
- a card key is a stable, skimmable global identifier;
- in person, an older adult collecting a child's prescription suggests a grandparent;
- later, controlled-substance ID laws and PBM signature or proof-of-delivery logs record identity, and for DMEPOS the relationship.

Insurer:
- AUTH_DOC paper checks reveal the patient's legal name tied to W_c, the representative's identity and the relationship;
- parentEdgeCount reveals single-parent households;
- custody and abuse revocations show up (its stored handle appears in the map, and its own revocations are distinguishable);
- the majority re-key date approximates the DOB;
- the origin of the co-sign request shows which family device holds the child's key;
- family history already gives it composition.

Sequencer:
- the same device and origin sign for W_p and W_c, and submit edges;
- E1 appears in the first epoch after W_c's public registration;
- insurer-signed revocations are distinguishable;
- the W_c -> W_a migration lands near the 18th birthday;
- if l2_submitDelegationEdge is wallet-signed (I7), it learns the grantor wallet for each leaf.

design.prescriber_indicates_dependent:
The prescriber indicates a dependent only by writing the prescription to the dependent's own wallet ID. memberWalletId = W_C in POST /prescriptions/write and /interactive/prescribe; the request shape is unchanged (src/nodes/routes/prescriber-routes.ts:425-448). The prescription carries:
- no dependent flag;
- no guardian, subscriber or cardholder field;
- no relationship code.
Compare NCPDP, which sends cardholder name 312-CC/313-CD and patient relationship 306-C6 on every dependent claim. SCRIPT BenefitsCoordination names the cardholder.

How the prescriber gets W_C: the parent's phone holds the custodial copy of W_C. It presents W_C's NFC attestation (it may ask for one by signing as W_C; src/l2/l2-node.ts:358-385) or a QR code.

B5 consent: the PCP assignment or the per-visit prescriber authorization is signed with W_C's key on the parent's phone (src/nodes/routes/member-routes.ts:1372-1400 → /interactive/prescriber-authorization). The engine therefore sees W_C consent and never sees the parent. Per-person accumulators, PA records and clinical checks stay keyed to one identity per person.

The only new prescriber input is an optional pickupLock, set when the visit falls under 164.502(g)(3)(i)(A)-(C). This means the minor consented alone, a court or another authorised person consented, or the parent agreed to confidentiality. The prescriber then does one of two things:
- scans the minor's self-key QR, so the lock records lockKeyHash;
- prints a one-time pickup slip for a minor without a phone.

The engine does not apply the lock by changing PrescriptionData. That would change every prescription circuit's verification key. Instead lockKeyHash is folded into rxBinding, so the engine and pharmacy both detect a stripped lock as a mismatch. While the lock is set, the prescriber node sends no member copy of the eScript to W_C's custodial key. The minor gets it in the room.

CONTROLLED is not a prescriber choice. The engine derives it from the NDC's schedule in the formulary.

What the prescriber learns:
- W_C, an opaque hash;
- the plan's price, under B3;
- whether it set pickupLock itself;
- for a locked prescription, the minor's self public key or the slip secret it printed (single use, bound to that prescription).

What it does not learn: the parent's or co-parent's wallet, any holder, the ticket set, who later picks up, family composition, the family hash or any relationship. Physically the prescriber still sees who brought the child in. That observation is outside zpk, and zpk adds nothing to it.
